== Concerning the Marks

#align(center)[Showing what are distinguishing Signs of truly gracious and holy Affections.]

I come now to the second thing proposed concerning the #emph[trial] of religious affections, namely, to take notice of some things in which affections that are spiritual and gracious differ from those that are not.

But before I go on directly to the distinguishing characteristics, I would first mention some things concerning the marks I will lay down, which I want to be noted.

1\. I am far from undertaking to give such signs of gracious affections as will be enough to enable anyone to distinguish with certainty true affection from false in others, or to determine positively which of their neighbors are true professing Christians and which are hypocrites. In doing that, I would be guilty of the very arrogance I have been condemning. It is plain that Christ has given rules to all Christians to enable them to judge of those who profess religion, whom they have to deal with, as far as is necessary for their own safety and to keep them from being led into a snare by false teachers and false pretenders to religion. It is also beyond doubt that the Scriptures abound with rules that may be very useful to ministers in counseling and guiding the souls committed to their care, in things that concern their spiritual and eternal state. Yet it is also evident that it was never God’s design to give us any rules by which we may know with certainty which of our fellow professing Christians are his, and so make a full and clear separation between sheep and goats. On the contrary, it was God’s design to reserve this to himself, as his prerogative. And therefore no distinguishing signs that will enable Christians or ministers to do this are ever to be expected, to the end of the world. For no more is ever to be expected from any signs found in the word of God, or gathered from it, than what Christ designed them for.

2\. No signs are to be expected that will be enough to enable those saints who are very low in grace, or who have departed far from God and have fallen into a dead, carnal and unchristian frame, to discern their own good state with certainty. It does not agree with God’s design (as has already been observed) that such people should know their good state. Nor is it desirable that they should; on the contrary, it is best in every way that they should not. And we have reason to bless God that he has made no provision for such people to know with certainty the state they are in, in any other way than by first coming out of the bad frame and way they are in.

Indeed, it is not properly because of any defect in the signs given in the word of God that not every living saint, whether strong or weak, and those in a bad frame as well as others, can know his good state with certainty by them. For the rules in themselves are certain and infallible, and every saint has, or has had, those things in himself that are sure evidences of grace; for #emph[every] act of grace, even the #emph[least,] is such an evidence. But it is because of a defect in the one to whom the signs are given. In the saint who is very low in grace, or in a bad frame, there is a twofold defect, which makes it impossible for him to know with certainty that he has true grace by the best signs and rules that can be given him. #emph[First,] there is a defect in the #emph[object,] that is, in the quality to be viewed and examined. I do not mean an essential defect, for I am supposing the person to be a real saint, but a defect in degree. Grace, being very small, cannot be clearly and certainly discerned and distinguished. When things are very small, we cannot clearly discern their form or distinguish them from one another, though in themselves their forms may be very different. There is no doubt a great difference between the body of a man and the bodies of other animals at the first conception in the womb. Yet if we were to view the different #emph[embryos,] we might not be able to discern the difference, because of the imperfect state of the object; but as it comes to greater perfection, the difference becomes very plain. The difference between creatures of very contrary qualities cannot be seen so plainly while they are very young, even after they are actually born, as in their more perfect state. The difference between doves and ravens, or doves and vultures, is not so evident when they first come out of the egg; but as they grow to their full perfection, it becomes exceedingly great and obvious. Another defect attending the grace of those I am speaking of is that it is mingled with so much corruption, which clouds and hides it and makes it impossible for it to be known with certainty. Different things in front of us may have in themselves many marks that thoroughly distinguish them from one another. Yet if we see them only in thick smoke, it may still be impossible to distinguish them. A fixed star is easily distinguished from a comet in a clear sky; but if we view them through a cloud, it may be impossible to see the difference. When true Christians are in a bad frame, guilt lies on the conscience. This brings fear, and so hinders the peace and joy of an assured hope.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[#emph[Secondly,] in such a case there is a defect in the #emph[eye.] As the feebleness of grace and the prevalence of corruption obscure the object, so they weaken the sight; they darken the sight with respect to all spiritual objects, of which grace is one. Sin is like some diseases of the eyes that make things appear in colors other than those that properly belong to them. It is like many other diseases that put the mouth out of taste, so that a man cannot distinguish good and wholesome food from bad, but everything tastes bitter. Men in a corrupt and carnal frame have their spiritual senses in a poor condition for judging and distinguishing spiritual things.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[For these reasons, no signs that can be given will actually satisfy people in such a case. However good and infallible the signs given may be, and however clearly they are laid down, they will not serve them. It is like giving a man rules for distinguishing visible objects in the dark. The things themselves may be very different, and their difference may be described to him very well and distinctly; yet all of it is insufficient to enable him to distinguish them, because he is in the dark. That is why many people in such a case spend their time in fruitless labor, poring over past experiences and examining themselves by signs they hear laid down from the pulpit or read in books, when there is other work for them to do, which is much more expected of them. While they neglect that work, all their self-examination is likely to be in vain, however much time they spend on it. The accursed thing must be destroyed from their camp, and #emph[Achan] must be put to death; and until this is done they will be in trouble. It is not God’s design that men should obtain assurance in any other way than by mortifying corruption, increasing in grace, and obtaining the lively exercise of it. And although self-examination is a duty of great use and importance, and by no means to be neglected, it is not the principal means by which the saints get satisfaction about their good state. Assurance is not to be obtained so much by #emph[self-examination] as by #emph[action.] The apostle #emph[Paul] sought assurance chiefly in this way, by #emph[forgetting what was behind, and reaching forward to what was ahead, pressing on toward the goal for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus, if by any means he might attain to the resurrection of the dead.] And it was chiefly by this means that he obtained assurance: “Therefore I do not run uncertainly” (1 Cor 9:26). He obtained assurance of winning the prize more by #emph[running] than by #emph[considering.] The #emph[swiftness of his pace] did more toward his assurance of victory than the #emph[strictness of his examination.] To give all diligence to grow in grace, by adding to faith virtue, #emph[and so on,] is the direction the apostle #emph[Peter] gives us for #emph[making our calling and election sure,] and having #emph[an entrance richly granted to us into Christ’s everlasting kingdom.] He is telling us that without this our eyes will be dim, and we will be like men in the dark who cannot plainly see things past or things to come, either the forgiveness of our past sins or our heavenly inheritance, which is future and #emph[far off] (2 Pet 1:5–11). #footnote[The way to know your godliness is to renew the visible exercises of grace.—The more the visible exercises of grace are renewed, the more certain you will be. The more frequently these actings are renewed, the more abiding and confirmed your assurance will be.—The more men’s grace is multiplied, the more their peace is multiplied (2 Pet 1:2): “Grace and peace be multiplied to you#super[+] through the knowledge of God and of Jesus our Lord.” #emph[Stoddard’s] way to know sincerity and hypocrisy, p. 139, and 142.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Therefore, good rules for distinguishing true grace from counterfeit may tend to convict hypocrites and be of great use to the saints in many respects. Among other benefits, they may be very useful to the saints in removing many needless scruples and establishing their hope. Yet I am far from claiming to lay down any rules that will be enough by themselves, without other means, to enable all true saints to see their good state, or from supposing that such rules should be the principal means of their satisfaction.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. Nor is there much encouragement, from the experience of present or past times, to lay down rules or marks for distinguishing true affections from false in hope of convincing any considerable number of that sort of hypocrites who have been deceived by great false discoveries and affections, and who are once settled in a false confidence and a high conceit of their own supposed great experiences and privileges. Such hypocrites are so conceited of their own wisdom, so blinded and hardened by a very great self-righteousness (though a very subtle and secret one, under the disguise of great humility), and so invincibly fond of their pleasing conceit of their own great exaltation, that it usually accomplishes nothing at all to lay before them the most convincing evidences of their hypocrisy. Their state is truly deplorable. Next to those who have committed the unpardonable sin, some of this sort of people seem to be furthest out of reach of the means of conviction and repentance. Yet laying down good rules may be a means of preventing such hypocrites, and of convincing many hypocrites of other kinds. And God is able to convince even this kind; his grace is not to be limited, and the means are not to be neglected. Besides, such rules may be of use to the true saints, to detect false affections that they may have mingled with true ones, and may be a means of their religion becoming more pure, like gold tried in the fire.]

Having laid these things down first, I now go on directly to take notice of the things in which true religious affections are distinguished from false.
